## Another big thing: fairness is finally explicit

The imported MVP has a proper sandbox concept:

```text
policy gets observation
↓
policy returns action
↓
timeout
↓
validator checks legality
↓
action applied
↓
physics resolves
```

That's exactly what we wanted from Robocode/OpenSpiel.

So I would lock this architecture:

```text
PLAYER POLICY
cannot touch physics

PLAYER POLICY
cannot mutate arbitrary engine state

PLAYER POLICY
only emits Action
```

Allowed action schema might be something like:

```text
steer(angle, intensity)
boost(intensity)
brake(intensity)
ability(id)
```

The simulator owns everything else.

This is what makes “all strat” credible.

## But there is one important contradiction we should resolve

The new brainstorm says:

> “Every battle, it learns.”

That is good for **between-match evolution**.

But our stronger game-theory mechanic says:

> During a match, the submitted policy is locked.

Those are compatible if we define:

```text
GENESIS
player seed frozen

BETWEEN MATCHES
agent may evolve legally

PRE-MATCH
agent analyzes opponent

COMMIT
battle policy hash locked

DURING MATCH
code fixed
internal state/memory may update

POST-MATCH
replay enters persistent history

NEXT MATCH
new policy version may be produced
```

That distinction needs to be canonical.

Otherwise “learning” becomes fuzzy.

